          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A post-menopausal woman undergoes DEXA scanning and her lowest T-score is −2.0 S</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A post-menopausal woman undergoes DEXA scanning and her lowest T-score is −2.0 SD. According to the WHO classification, how should this result be interpreted?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Normal bone mineral density</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A T-score at or above −1.0 SD is classified as normal. A score of −2.0 falls well below the normal threshold.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteopenia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A T-score between −1.0 and −2.5 SD defines osteopenia — a state of reduced bone mass that indicates increased fracture risk, but has not reached the diagnostic threshold for osteoporosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteoporosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Osteoporosis requires a T-score at or below −2.5 SD. At −2.0, the patient has reduced bone mass but has not crossed the osteoporosis threshold.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Severe osteoporosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Severe (established) osteoporosis requires a T-score ≤ −2.5 SD together with one or more fragility fractures. Neither criterion is met here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The WHO DEXA thresholds are: normal ≥ −1.0 SD, osteopenia −1.0 to −2.5 SD, osteoporosis ≤ −2.5 SD. A score of −2.0 falls in the osteopenia range."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Osteopenia (T-score −1.0 to −2.5 SD) identifies patients who benefit from lifestyle modification, calcium and vitamin D supplementation, and monitoring for progression to osteoporosis."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
