          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Craniotabes is recognised as the earliest clinical sign of rickets in infants. W</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Craniotabes is recognised as the earliest clinical sign of rickets in infants. What is the underlying mechanism that produces the characteristic 'ping-pong ball' sensation on pressing the occiput?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Subperiosteal osteoid accumulation causing skull vault thickening</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Subperiosteal osteoid accumulation produces frontal and parietal bossing (the 'squarish skull') — a later sign. It causes thickening, not the softening felt in craniotabes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Demineralisation of the thin occipital and posterior parietal bones</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The occipital and posterior parietal bones are among the thinnest in the infant skull. Defective mineralisation softens these bones earliest, producing the springy, ping-pong ball sensation on palpation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Premature closure of the posterior fontanelle</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Rickets delays fontanelle closure rather than accelerating it. A wide, late-closing fontanelle is a feature of rickets, not premature closure.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cartilaginous expansion at costochondral junctions</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cartilaginous expansion at costochondral junctions produces the rachitic rosary — a different sign located on the chest wall, not the skull.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Craniotabes occurs because the thin occipital and posterior parietal bones are most vulnerable to demineralisation in rickets, becoming soft and springy on palpation — like pressing a ping-pong ball."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent. Craniotabes reflects the earliest impact of defective mineralisation on the thinnest skull bones — always examine for it in at-risk infants before more obvious signs like bossing or rosary appear."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
