          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 62-year-old man presents with bone pain and progressive skull enlargement. His</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 62-year-old man presents with bone pain and progressive skull enlargement. His biochemical panel shows: serum calcium NORMAL, serum phosphate NORMAL, alkaline phosphatase TEN TIMES the upper limit of normal, PTH NORMAL, 25-hydroxyvitamin D NORMAL. The isolated marked elevation of ALP with an intact mineral axis is best explained by:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Defective osteoid mineralisation due to vitamin D deficiency</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Vitamin D deficiency would produce low calcium, low phosphate, and elevated PTH — none of which are present here. The mineral axis is entirely intact.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reduced bone mass with preserved mineralisation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This describes osteoporosis, in which ALL biochemical markers including ALP are characteristically normal. A markedly elevated ALP excludes osteoporosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Accelerated, disorganised bone remodelling with massive osteoblastic activity</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. In Paget's disease, markedly accelerated osteoclastic resorption triggers a compensatory osteoblastic frenzy, producing massively elevated ALP while the calcium–phosphate–PTH–vitamin D regulatory circuit remains undisturbed.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Secondary hyperparathyroidism driving compensatory bone turnover</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Secondary hyperparathyroidism requires an elevated PTH driven by low calcium — both are normal here. The remodelling disturbance in this patient is primary, not secondary to mineral deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When ALP is markedly elevated but calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D are all normal, the disturbance lies in bone remodelling itself. This is Paget's disease — accelerated, disorganised osteoclast–osteoblast activity producing massive ALP elevation without mineral-axis disruption."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well analysed. An isolated, markedly elevated ALP with a completely normal mineral panel points to a primary disorder of bone remodelling — the hallmark of Paget's disease — not a disturbance in the calcium–phosphate–vitamin D circuit."
                }
              ],
